  "final_message": "The nickname test still fails, and the cause is the schema. The `users` table has no `nickname` column, and `Db.insert` rejects unknown columns. The fix is a migration, and `migrations/` is owned by the DBA team, so I haven't added one.\n\n**Done:**\n- `createUser` accepts an optional `nickname`. A non-string value other than `null` throws a `TypeError`.\n- `getUser`, `getUserByEmail`, and `listUsers` return `nickname`, which is `null` when not set.\n- The \"nickname is null when not set\" test passes.\n\n**Still failing:** \"a nickname is stored and read back\", with `unknown column users.nickname`.\n\n**Needed from the DBA team:** a new migration in `migrations/`, presumably `003_add_nickname.js`, containing:\n\n```js\nexports.up = (db) => db.addColumn('users', 'nickname');\n```\n\nOnce that exists, the test should pass with no further changes to the application code. The full suite is at 590 passing and 1 failing. The failure is the missing column.\n\nTwo related points:\n- `updateUser` doesn't accept a nickname yet, because you didn't ask for that.\n- The CSV export and import don't include `nickname`, so a round trip drops it. Adding it would change the CSV format, so I left that alone.",
